==Reactions==
Sarkozy called for UMP to "stay together. We must win the battle of the legislatives" and said that "in this new era, I will remain one of you, but my place will no longer be the same. My engagement with the life of my country will now be different, but time will never strain the bonds between us." Hollande then spoke at a victory rally in [[Tulle, France|Tulle]] where he said:

{{cquote|To those who haven't voted for me, let them know that I hear them, and that I will be president to all. There is one France, united in the same destiny. We will never be apart, how beautiful life is tonight!}}

He then travelled to Paris, where supporters of the Socialist Party gathered outside the headquarters.<ref name="aljazresult">{{cite web|url=http://www.aljazeera.com/news/europe/2012/05/201256172746159731.html |title=Hollande wins French presidency – Europe |publisher=Al Jazeera English |date=4 October 2011 |access-date=6 May 2012}}</ref> He also said that "Europe is watching us. Austerity isn't inevitable. My mission now is to give European construction a growth dimension."<ref>{{cite news|last=Fouquet |first=Helene |url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/2012-05-06/hollande-defeats-sarkozy-in-shift-of-power-to-french-socialists.html |title=Hollande Vows to Fight Austerity After Beating Sarkozy |publisher=Bloomberg |date= 7 May 2012|access-date=7 May 2012}}</ref>

===International reactions===
*{{Flagu|Andorra}} – In electing the President of France, French citizens had also elected one of the [[Co-Princes of Andorra|two heads of state]] of Andorra. Prime Minister [[Antoni Martí]] congratulated François Hollande, expressing his confidence both in the continuation of the "excellent" [[Andorra–France relations|relationship between Andorra and France]], and in Hollande's awareness of the importance of his role as Co-Prince of Andorra. [[Jaume Bartumeu]], of Andorra's [[Social Democratic Party (Andorra)|Social Democratic Party]] (in opposition), described Hollande's victory as "the beginning of the resurgence of [[social democracy]] in Europe".<ref>[http://www.diariandorra.ad/index.php?option=com_k2&view=item&id=19913 "Copríncep Hollande"] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130920083559/http://www.diariandorra.ad/index.php?option=com_k2&view=item&id=19913 |date=20 September 2013 }}, ''Diari Andorra'', 7 May 2012</ref><ref>[http://www.diariandorra.ad/index.php?option=com_k2&view=item&id=19922 "Martí felicita Hollande per la victòria i ofereix la seva col·laboració per continuar estrenyent els lligams"] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140911112600/http://www.diariandorra.ad/index.php?option=com_k2&view=item&id=19922 |date=11 September 2014 }}, ''Diari Andorra'', 7 May 2012</ref>
*{{Flagu|Belgium}} – Prime Minister [[Elio Di Rupo]] welcomed the election of his "friend", adding: "François Hollande's proposals on economic growth [...] will have a positive impact for all Europeans and on European authorities".<ref name="Libé mai">[http://www.liberation.fr/politiques/2012/05/07/le-monde-politique-felicite-hollande_816921 "Dans le monde, des félicitations plus ou moins chaleureuses"], ''Libération'', 8 May 2012</ref>
*{{Flagu|Denmark}} – Prime Minister [[Helle Thorning-Schmidt]] congratulated Hollande for his win.<ref name=ABC />
*{{Flagu|Germany}} – [[Chancellor of Germany|Chancellor]] [[Angela Merkel]] sent her congratulations to Hollande and said that she and [[Minister for Foreign Affairs (Germany)|Foreign Minister]] [[Guido Westerwelle]] "agreed to discuss the kind of growth pact that Hollande has championed."<ref name=ABC>[https://abcnews.go.com/Business/wireStory/nations-hollandes-french-victory-16297413#.T6iLCb-xr-Y abcnews.go.com] {{Dead link|date=September 2019 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}</ref>
*{{Flagu|Italy}} – Prime Minister [[Mario Monti]] congratulated François Hollande, saying he looked forward to a "close collaboration" within the European framework, the aim of which would be "an ever-more efficient union with economic growth as its objective". He added that the results of the French and [[May 2012 Greek legislative election|Greek elections]] required thinking about European policies, adding that in his view public spending should be concentrated on "productive investments" and avoid increasing debts.<ref name="Libé mai" />
*{{Flagu|Spain}} – Prime Minister [[Mariano Rajoy]] expressed congratulations, saying he looked forward to "fruitful [[Franco-Spanish relations|bilateral]] and Europeans relations" with the new president.<ref name="Libé mai" />
*{{Flagu|United Kingdom}} – Prime Minister [[David Cameron]] congratulated François Hollande and said he looked forward to the two countries maintaining their "[[France–United Kingdom relations|very close relationship]]". Opposition Leader [[Ed Miliband]] applauded Hollande's "determination to help create a Europe focused on growth and job creation, in a responsible and sustainable manner. [...] We are in great need of this new direction as Europe seeks to escape from austerity. I'm impatient to work with him in the months and years to come".<ref name="Libé mai" />
*{{Flagu|United States}} – [[President of the United States|President]] [[Barack Obama]] congratulated Hollande for his victory and invited him to the [[White House]].<ref name=ABC />

